    <string name="settings_about">About Atlas</string>
    <string name="settings_how">How it works</string>
    <string name="settings_how_text">Atlas sees which names your apps look up and which addresses they connect to. It matches them against a list of companies that is part of the system. Nothing leaves the phone.</string>
    <string name="settings_cannot_see">What Atlas cannot see</string>
    <string name="cannot_1">How much data an app sent or received</string>
    <string name="cannot_2">Apps that connect to a fixed address without a name lookup</string>
    <string name="cannot_3">Apps that look up names themselves, with DNS over HTTPS inside the app</string>
    <string name="cannot_4">Services that are not in the list yet</string>
    <string name="cannot_5">Traffic sent over UDP</string>
    <string name="settings_private">Everything stays on this phone. Atlas never sends what it sees anywhere.</string>
    <string name="data_sources">Company data: AdGuard Companies DB (CC BY-SA 4.0, built on whotracks.me, CC BY 4.0) and Wikidata (CC0), with facts from company filings. See NOTICE in the classifier. Server places: country and operator from ip-location-db (PDDL), anycast prefixes from the University of Twente census (MPL-2.0), city from IP Geolocation by DB-IP (CC BY 4.0, db-ip.com).</string>

    <string name="delete_title">Delete everything collected?</string>
    <string name="delete_text">The history and the counts on this phone are removed. This cannot be undone.</string>
    <string name="export_title">Export the services your apps contact?</string>
    <string name="export_text">The file lists, for each app, the services it contacted, how often and when, for the 2000 most recent entries at most. A server reached by address alone is listed by that address, and the apps are named by their package. It leaves Atlas: the place you choose can keep it, and so can any app that reads it.</string>
